  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/... \nA much more active weather pattern is shaping up to take place by\nthe middle portion of the week as a deep upper-level low \namplifies off the CA coast and then moves inland over the Desert \nSouthwest through the remainder of the work week. Latest ensemble \nand deterministic model suite have come into a better agreement in\nshowing a deep upper-level low with strong negative height \nanomalies becoming established off the southern CA coast on \nWednesday and potentially obtaining a slight negative tilt. This \nwill cause the flow aloft to become established more out of the \nsouth, advecting deeper moisture northward into the region \nstarting Tuesday night. In fact, both the EPS and GEFS show PWAT \nvalues peaking above 250% of normal with the North American \nEnsemble Forecasting System depicting moisture levels approaching \nnear record values for mid-October. In addition, the combination \nof the deeper moisture as well as steeper mid-level lapse rates \nwill likely lead to significant instability with both the ECMWF \nand GFS indicating MUCAPE values approaching 1000 J/KG. Therefore,\nthe combination of the strong synoptic ascent from the trough and\nfavorable thermodynamic parameters will likely lead to multiple \nrounds of showers and thunderstorms to break out across much of \nsouth-central AZ Wednesday through Thursday afternoon, period when\nrainfall chances will be highest, while much of the western \ndeserts should remain mostly dry given that the greatest moisture \nadvection will occur mostly over central and eastern AZ. \n\nWith the moderate levels of instability likely to be in place, any \nshower and thunderstorm activity will be capable of producing \nrainfall rates in excess of 1-2\"/hr with training of activity likely \nto be an issue. Thus, there will be a heightened potential for flash \nflooding, especially across the foothills and higher terrain areas \nto the north and east of Phoenix where upslope flow will likely \nenhance the activity. Given this heightened potential for flash \nflooding, the Weather Prediction Center has placed areas from \nPhoenix and points north and east in a slight risk for excessive \nrainfall leading to flash flooding for both Wednesday and Thursday. \nIn terms of rainfall amounts, the latest forecast from the Weather \nPrediction Center has much of the foothills and higher terrain areas \nnorth and east of Phoenix potentially receiving upwards of 1.00-\n2.50\" while across the lower deserts, including the Phoenix metro, \namounts are likely to be lower ranging between 0.25-1.00\", although \nlocally higher amounts will be likely in areas that see training of \nactivity. In addition to the heavy rainfall threat, given that the\n0-6 km shear will be in excess of 50 kts combined with the \nmoderate levels of instability, there will also be the threat of a\nfew strong to severe storms capable of producing strong wind \ngusts and hail. \n\nAs the upper-level low moves inland into the southern Great Basin \nlate Thursday, strong dry air advection will gradually push eastward \nending rain chances by Thursday afternoon across the south-central \nAZ lower deserts with rain chances lingering into early Friday over \nthe higher terrain. There are indications from guidance of a\nfollow-on shortwave moving through area late Friday through \nSaturday, however, it should be inconsequential given that there \nwill be an abundance of dry air in place limiting any potential \nfor additional rainfall.\n\nUnder the influence of the general troughing pattern as well as the \nheightened rain chances, temperatures by the latter half of the week \nwill rapidly cool off towards readings that will be several degrees \nbelow normal. The latest NBM shows much of the lower deserts \nstruggling to reach 80 degrees for afternoon highs both Thursday and \nFriday, with Friday likely to be coolest day. Temperatures will \ngradually warm up next weekend but still remain below normal.",
